First, let's find the values of $x$ and $y$ in the parametric form of the given equation:

$$
\left\{
\begin{array}{l}
x = 3\cos\theta \\
y = \sqrt{7}\sin\theta
\end{array}
\right.
$$

The distances $|AF_{1}|$ and $|AF_{2}|$ are:

$$
\begin{align*}
|AF_{1}| &= 3\cos{\theta} + 3 \\
|AF_{2}| &= \sqrt{7}\sin{\theta} + \sqrt{7}
\end{align*}
$$

Let the area of triangle $A\,F_{1}F_{2}$ be $S$:

$$
\begin{align*}
S &= \frac{1}{2}|F_{1}F_{2}|\left|\, AF_{1}\sin{45^{\circ}}\,\right| \\
&= 3\sqrt{7}\cdot|\sin{\theta}+\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}|
\end{align*}
$$

At $y = 1$, the area is maximized:

$$
\begin{align*}
\frac{S}{9} &= 1 \\
\frac{\pi}{8} &= \theta \\
\frac{7\sqrt{7}}{8} &\geq |\sin{\theta}+\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}| \\
S &\leq \boxed{\frac{21\sqrt{14}}{8}}
\end{align*}
$$

Therefore, the maximum value of the area of triangle $A\,F_{1}F_{2}$ is $S = \boxed{\frac{21\sqrt{14}}{8}}$.

$$Answer: \boxed{\frac{21\sqrt{14}}{8}}$$

