 To solve this problem, we need to determine the determinant of the matrix whose columns are $\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b},$ $\mathbf{b} + \mathbf{c},$ and $\mathbf{c} + \mathbf{a},$ in terms of the determinant $D$ of the matrix whose columns are $\mathbf{a},$ $\mathbf{b},$ and $\mathbf{c}.$ Let's denote this new matrix as $M.$ We can express $\mathbf{a},$ $\mathbf{b},$ and $\mathbf{c}$ in terms of the columns of $M.$
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{a} &= (\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b}) - (\mathbf{b} + \mathbf{c}), \\
\mathbf{b} &= (\mathbf{b} + \mathbf{c}) - (\mathbf{c} + \mathbf{a}), \\
\mathbf{c} &= (\mathbf{c} + \mathbf{a}) - (\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b}).
\end{align*}
Now we can take the determinant of $M$ by expanding it along the first row:
\begin{align*}
\det(M) &= \begin{vmatrix}
    \mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b} & \mathbf{b} + \mathbf{c} & \mathbf{c} + \mathbf{a} \\
    \mathbf{a} & \mathbf{b} & \mathbf{c} \\
    \mathbf{a} & \mathbf{b} & \mathbf{c}
\end{vmatrix} \\
&= \begin{vmatrix}
    (\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b}) - (\mathbf{b} + \mathbf{c}) & \mathbf{b} + \mathbf{c} & \mathbf{c} + \mathbf{a} \\
    \mathbf{a} & \mathbf{b} & \mathbf{c} \\
    \mathbf{a} & \mathbf{b} & \mathbf{c}
\end{vmatrix} \\
&= \begin{vmatrix}
    \mathbf{a} & \mathbf{b} & \mathbf{c} \\
    \mathbf{a} & \mathbf{b} & \mathbf{c}
\end{vmatrix} - \begin{vmatrix}
    (\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b}) - (\mathbf{b} + \mathbf{c}) & \mathbf{b} + \mathbf{c} & \mathbf{c} + \mathbf{a} \\
    \mathbf{a} & \mathbf{b} & \mathbf{c} \\
    \mathbf{a} & \mathbf{b} & \mathbf{c}
\end{vmatrix}.
\end{align*}
The second determinant is equal to $0$ because two rows are identical. Therefore, we have
\begin{align*}
\det(M) &= \begin{vmatrix}
    \mathbf{a} & \mathbf{b} & \mathbf{c} \\
    \mathbf{a} & \mathbf{b} & \mathbf{c}
\end{vmatrix}.
\end{align*}
Now, let's compute the determinant of this matrix:
\begin{align*}
\det(M) &= \begin{vmatrix}
    \mathbf{a} & \mathbf{b} & \mathbf{c} \\
    \mathbf{a} & \mathbf{b} & \mathbf{c}
\end{vmatrix} \\
&= \begin{vmatrix}
    \mathbf{a} & \mathbf{b} & \mathbf{c} \\
    \mathbf{a} & \mathbf{b} & \mathbf{c}
\end{vmatrix} - \begin{vmatrix}
    \mathbf{a} & \mathbf{b} & \mathbf{c} \\
    \mathbf{a} & \mathbf{b} & \mathbf{c}
\end{vmatrix} \\
&= 0.
\end{align*}
Notice that since $\mathbf{a},$ $\mathbf{b},$ and $\mathbf{c}$ are linearly dependent, their columns are also linearly dependent. This means that the matrix whose columns are $\mathbf{a},$ $\mathbf{b},$ and $\mathbf{c}$ has rank $1$ and hence its determinant is $0.$ Therefore, the determinant of the matrix whose columns are $\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b},$ $\mathbf{b} + \mathbf{c},$ and $\mathbf{c} + \mathbf{a}$ is $\boxed{0}.$

